{ Let's now look at the stonewall attack against other defenses.

Make the first moves }
1. d4 Nf6 { [%csl Ge3][%cal Ge2e3] } 2. e3 g6 { Now Black performs the King's Indian defense
Let's see how to play here } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf1d3] } 3. Bd3 Bg7 { Play f4 } { [%csl Gf4][%cal Gf2f4] } 4. f4 O-O { Now Nf3 } { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 5. Nf3 d6 { Against this defense, our plan to bring the knight to e5 is not possible.
Now you must actively play with e4 } { [%csl Ge4,Re5][%cal Ge3e4,Rd6e5] } 6. e4 { Look at that beautiful chain of pawns!
See how many squares they control! } { [%csl Bc5,Bd5,Be5,Bf5,Bg5][%cal Bf4e5,Bd4e5,Be4f5,Bf4g5,Be4d5,Bd4c5] } 6... c5 { Black now makes c5 to end our pawn chain
Capture the pawn } 7. dxc5 dxc5 { Now play e5 } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } 8. e5 Nd5 (8... Nh5 { And now play Nc3 } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 9. Nc3 { We have a good game }) *

